exec(open('/home/claude/work/gita/gc.py').read())
KUR='#7a2a6a'; PAN='#1d6fb8'
FS=lambda lab,extra: sc(lab,FIELD+extra)
V=[
X(1,'WHAT DID THEY DO?',
 FS('A blind old king turning toward his minister, who glows with divine sight and sees the battlefield far away',KINGB(60,130,1.3)+SANJ(160,130,1.3)+'<ellipse cx="160" cy="40" rx="50" ry="24" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+ARMY(124,56,4,KUR,10,.35)+ARMY(164,56,4,PAN,10,.35)),
 'Dhṛtarāṣṭra said: On the field of dharma, the field of the Kurus, gathered and eager to fight, what did my sons and the sons of Pāṇḍu do, Sañjaya?',
 [('dhṛtarāṣṭra uvāca','<b>Dhṛtarāṣṭra said</b>'),('dharma-kṣetre','<b>on the field of dharma</b>'),('kuru-kṣetre','<b>at Kurukṣetra</b>'),('samavetāḥ','<b>gathered together</b>'),('yuyutsavaḥ','<b>eager to fight</b>'),('māmakāḥ · pāṇḍavāḥ','<b>mine · Pāṇḍu’s sons</b>')],
 'The war is already ten days old when Sañjaya returns to tell the king that Bhīṣma has fallen; in the epic, the Gītā is told as part of that report. Dhṛtarāṣṭra, blind from birth, never goes to the field. This is the only verse he speaks in the whole Gītā.',
 'Two words carry the weight. <i>Dharma-kṣetra</i>: Kurukṣetra was an ancient holy place of sacrifice, so the battle is set on sacred ground. And <i>māmakāḥ</i>, “mine”: the king divides the family into “my sons” and “Pāṇḍu’s sons” in his first breath. Many teachers, Gandhi among them, read the whole war as the inner battle between good and bad in each of us.',
 'Every day we stand on a field of dharma, where right and wrong must be chosen. Notice how quickly the mind says “mine” and “theirs”. That small word sits at the root of most family quarrels, and Dhṛtarāṣṭra shows where it can lead.',
 'The Gītā opens with <b>“mine” against “theirs”</b>.',
 ("Why start with a blind king?","Many read his blindness as a picture of attachment: he can't see right from wrong when it comes to his own sons.")),
X(2,'DURYODHANA GOES TO DROṆA',
 FS('A proud prince in purple striding toward an old teacher with a white beard, the enemy army behind',KING(60,130,1.1)+SAGE(110,130,1.1)+ARMY(140,130,4,PAN,18,.55)),
 'Sañjaya said: Then King Duryodhana, seeing the army of the Pāṇḍavas drawn up in battle order, went up to his teacher (Droṇa) and spoke these words.',
 [('sañjaya uvāca','<b>Sañjaya said</b>'),('dṛṣṭvā','<b>having seen</b>'),('pāṇḍava-anīkam','<b>the Pāṇḍava army</b>'),('vyūḍham','<b>arrayed in formation</b>'),('ācāryam upasaṅgamya','<b>going up to the teacher</b>'),('rājā','<b>the king</b>: Duryodhana')],
 'Sañjaya begins his report. Duryodhana, the eldest Kaurava and the real force behind the war, looks across and sees the Pāṇḍava army in formation. He goes not to Bhīṣma, his commander-in-chief, but to Droṇa, who taught all the princes on both sides.',
 'Sañjaya calls Duryodhana <i>rājā</i>, “king”, though his father still sits on the throne: it shows who really rules. The verb <i>dṛṣṭvā</i>, “having seen”, is worth marking. In chapter 1 both Duryodhana and Arjuna react to what they see. One sees enemies and plans; the other will see family and collapse.',
 'What we see depends on what is in us. The same field looks like a threat to one man and a tragedy to another. Before reacting, it helps to ask: what am I actually seeing, and what am I bringing to it?',
 'Duryodhana sees the enemy <b>and runs to his teacher</b>.'),
X(3,'“YOUR OWN PUPIL”',
 FS('Duryodhana pointing across the field at a young commander, a dotted line connecting the commander back to old Drona',KING(50,130,1)+SAGE(90,130,1)+person(180,130,PAN,'#c98b5a',1)+'<path d="M100 90 Q140 40 176 90" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/>'+T(140,40,'YOUR PUPIL!',10)),
 '“See, teacher, this mighty army of the sons of Pāṇḍu, arrayed by the son of Drupada, your own clever pupil.”',
 [('paśya','<b>see!</b>'),('etām mahatīm camūm','<b>this great army</b>'),('drupada-putreṇa','<b>by Drupada’s son</b>'),('tava śiṣyeṇa','<b>by your pupil</b>'),('dhīmatā','<b>clever, intelligent</b>')],
 'The Pāṇḍava commander is Dhṛṣṭadyumna, son of King Drupada. Drupada and Droṇa were boyhood friends who became bitter enemies; Drupada performed a sacrifice to get a son who would kill Droṇa. Droṇa knew this and still taught the boy archery.',
 'Duryodhana’s words carry a sting. “Your own pupil” reminds Droṇa that his generosity armed his future killer, and hints: do not be soft on the Pāṇḍavas, who are also your pupils. Readers have long heard fear and suspicion behind the polite words. A leader who trusts his people does not needle them on the eve of battle.',
 'Notice when we speak to others to manage our own anxiety. Duryodhana’s jab is really about his nerves. Honest words build a team; barbed words, even clever ones, weaken it.',
 'A polite sentence <b>with a sting in it</b>.'),
X(4,'MIGHTY ARCHERS',
 FS('Rows of Pandava heroes with bows; names written above: Bhima, Arjuna, Yuyudhana, Virata, Drupada',ARMY(14,130,6,PAN,32,.8)+T(110,30,'BHĪMA · ARJUNA · YUYUDHĀNA · VIRĀṬA · DRUPADA',8)),
 '“Here are heroes, mighty archers, equal in battle to Bhīma and Arjuna: Yuyudhāna, Virāṭa, and Drupada, the great chariot-warrior;”',
 [('atra śūrāḥ','<b>here are heroes</b>'),('mahā-iṣvāsāḥ','<b>great archers</b>'),('bhīma-arjuna-samāḥ','<b>equal to Bhīma and Arjuna</b>'),('yuyudhānaḥ','<b>Yuyudhāna</b>, also called Sātyaki'),('mahā-rathaḥ','<b>great chariot-warrior</b>')],
 'Duryodhana begins a roll call of the Pāṇḍava side. Yuyudhāna (Sātyaki) is a Yādava warrior, Kṛṣṇa’s kinsman and Arjuna’s student. Virāṭa is the king who sheltered the Pāṇḍavas in their year of hiding. Drupada is the father of Draupadī and Dhṛṣṭadyumna.',
 'A <i>mahāratha</i> was a rank: in the epic’s reckoning, a warrior able to fight ten thousand archers alone. Duryodhana measures everyone against Bhīma and Arjuna; those two clearly haunt him. Bhīma had vowed to kill all of Dhṛtarāṣṭra’s sons, and Arjuna is the best archer alive.',
 'Our fears show in what we count. Duryodhana keeps listing the other side’s strengths. Listing risks is wise; but if the list never ends, it is worry, not planning.',
 'He counts the enemy’s heroes <b>one by one</b>.'),
X(5,'MORE NAMES ON THE OTHER SIDE',
 FS('Three more Pandava allies with crowns: Dhrishtaketu, Chekitana and the king of Kashi',KING(50,130,1)+KING(110,130,1)+KING(170,130,1)+T(110,30,'DHṚṢṬAKETU · CEKITĀNA · KĀŚIRĀJA',8)),
 '“Dhṛṣṭaketu, Cekitāna and the valiant king of Kāśī; Purujit, Kuntibhoja and Śaibya, the bull among men;”',
 [('dhṛṣṭaketuḥ','<b>Dhṛṣṭaketu</b>, king of the Cedis'),('kāśi-rājaḥ','<b>the king of Kāśī</b>'),('vīryavān','<b>valiant</b>'),('kuntibhojaḥ','<b>Kuntibhoja</b>, Kuntī’s foster-father'),('nara-puṅgavaḥ','<b>bull among men</b>')],
 'The list goes on. Dhṛṣṭaketu was the son of Śiśupāla, whom Kṛṣṇa had killed, yet he fights on Kṛṣṇa’s side. Kuntibhoja adopted Kuntī, mother of the Pāṇḍavas; Purujit is his relative. Śaibya was a king of the Śibis.',
 'The Pāṇḍava side is a coalition of kingdoms joined by marriage, gratitude and principle. Kings came from Kāśī (Vārāṇasī) and beyond. The list reminds us that this was a war of all ancient India: almost every royal house had to choose a side.',
 'Big conflicts pull in everyone connected. Family quarrels spread to friends and in-laws. Choosing sides is often forced on people, and they carry the cost too.',
 'A whole land <b>chose sides</b>.'),
X(6,'THE YOUNG WARRIORS',
 FS('Young warriors in bright colours: Abhimanyu and the five sons of Draupadi',''.join(person(30+k*32,130,c,'#c98b5a',.8) for k,c in enumerate(['#ffd60a','#c0392b','#2a9d6f','#1d6fb8','#ff8c1a','#9b5de5']))+T(110,30,'ABHIMANYU · DRAUPADEYAS',10)),
 '“Yudhāmanyu the bold and Uttamaujā the mighty; the son of Subhadrā, and the sons of Draupadī: all of them great chariot-warriors.”',
 [('vikrāntaḥ','<b>bold, valiant</b>'),('vīryavān','<b>mighty</b>'),('saubhadraḥ','<b>son of Subhadrā</b>: Abhimanyu'),('draupadeyāḥ','<b>sons of Draupadī</b>'),('sarve eva mahārathāḥ','<b>all great chariot-warriors</b>')],
 'Yudhāmanyu and Uttamaujā are Pāñcāla princes who guarded Arjuna’s chariot wheels. Abhimanyu is Arjuna’s young son by Subhadrā, Kṛṣṇa’s sister. The five sons of Draupadī, one by each Pāṇḍava, are also young.',
 'This list is heartbreaking to anyone who knows the epic. Abhimanyu will be trapped and killed in the cakravyūha on the thirteenth day; the sons of Draupadī will be killed in their sleep after the war ends. Duryodhana names them as threats; the Mahābhārata remembers them as the war’s young victims.',
 'Wars between adults are paid for by the young. Before any conflict, at home or in the world, it is worth asking who will pay for it in the end.',
 'The young names <b>the war will take</b>.'),
X(7,'“NOW HEAR OUR LEADERS”',
 FS('Duryodhana turning to his own side, holding up a list, a purple army behind him',KING(60,130,1.2)+'<rect x="80" y="70" width="30" height="40" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+ARMY(120,130,5,KUR,18,.6)),
 '“But know also, best of the twice-born, the leaders of my army, the most distinguished among us. I name them for your information.”',
 [('asmākam tu','<b>but ours</b>'),('viśiṣṭāḥ','<b>the most distinguished</b>'),('nibodha','<b>take note</b>'),('dvija-uttama','<b>best of the twice-born</b>: Droṇa'),('saṃjñā-artham','<b>for your information</b>')],
 'Having listed the enemy, Duryodhana turns to his own commanders. He calls Droṇa <i>dvijottama</i>, “best of Brahmins”. Droṇa was a Brahmin by birth who lived as a warrior and teacher of arms.',
 'Telling Droṇa “for your information” who his own colleagues are is odd: he knows them all. Readers see a nervous man talking to calm himself. The title “best of Brahmins” may also hint that Droṇa, a Brahmin, should not turn soft-hearted.',
 'When anxious, we often talk too much and explain what everyone already knows. Spotting it in ourselves is the first step to calm.',
 'Duryodhana talks <b>to calm himself</b>.'),
X(8,'THE KAURAVA HEROES',
 FS('Kaurava heroes in a row with names above: Bhishma, Karna, Kripa, Ashvatthama',SAGE(30,130,.9)+SAGE(70,130,.9)+KING(110,130,.9)+SAGE(150,130,.9)+KING(190,130,.9)+T(110,30,'BHĪṢMA · KARṆA · KṚPA · AŚVATTHĀMĀ',8)),
 '“Yourself, Bhīṣma, Karṇa, and Kṛpa, the victor in battle; Aśvatthāmā, Vikarṇa, and the son of Somadatta (Bhūriśravas);”',
 [('bhavān','<b>yourself</b>: Droṇa'),('karṇaḥ','<b>Karṇa</b>'),('kṛpaḥ samitiñjayaḥ','<b>Kṛpa, victorious in war</b>'),('aśvatthāmā','<b>Aśvatthāmā</b>, Droṇa’s son'),('saumadattiḥ','<b>son of Somadatta</b>')],
 'Duryodhana names Droṇa first, out of courtesy, then Bhīṣma. Karṇa, his closest friend, is secretly Kuntī’s eldest son, a brother of the Pāṇḍavas. Kṛpa is the family’s other teacher; Aśvatthāmā is Droṇa’s son; Vikarṇa is a Kaurava brother who once spoke up for Draupadī.',
 'Almost every name on this list is tied to the Pāṇḍavas: teachers, a great-uncle, a hidden brother. Vikarṇa alone protested Draupadī’s humiliation, yet he fights for his brother. The Mahābhārata does not show simple good and bad sides; it shows people bound by loyalty, sometimes to the wrong cause.',
 'Loyalty is a virtue until it binds us to a wrong. Vikarṇa and Karṇa both knew better and stayed. Where are we loyal to people over what is right?',
 'Good men <b>on the wrong side</b>.'),
X(9,'READY TO DIE FOR ME',
 FS('A crowd of Kaurava warriors with many weapons raised',ARMY(10,130,10,KUR,20,.6)+T(110,30,'MANY WEAPONS, ONE MASTER',10)),
 '“And many other heroes who have given up their lives for my sake, armed with many weapons, all skilled in war.”',
 [('anye ca bahavaḥ śūrāḥ','<b>and many other heroes</b>'),('mad-arthe','<b>for my sake</b>'),('tyakta-jīvitāḥ','<b>who have given up their lives</b>'),('nānā-śastra-praharaṇāḥ','<b>armed with many weapons</b>'),('yuddha-viśāradāḥ','<b>skilled in war</b>')],
 'Duryodhana closes his list: countless others are ready to die for him. In the epic’s count, the Kaurava side had more allies and a larger army.',
 'The phrase <i>mad-arthe</i>, “for my sake”, shows how Duryodhana sees the war: it is about him. The Pāṇḍavas, in their telling, fought for their rightful share and for dharma; Duryodhana sees thousands giving their lives for him. Some readers also hear <i>tyakta-jīvitāḥ</i> darkly: “as good as dead already”.',
 'Be wary of any cause where the leader says “for my sake”. Ask: who is this really for?',
 'Many ready to die <b>“for me”</b>.'),
X(10,'BOAST OR FEAR?',
 FS('A big purple army behind a smaller blue one, a question mark between them',ARMY(10,130,6,KUR,16,.5)+ARMY(130,130,4,PAN,18,.5)+T(55,40,'BHĪṢMA',11)+T(165,40,'BHĪMA',11)+T(110,90,'?',24)),
 '“Our army, guarded by Bhīṣma, is unlimited (or: insufficient); while their army, guarded by Bhīma, is limited (or: sufficient).”',
 [('aparyāptam','<b>unlimited</b>, or <b>insufficient</b>'),('tat asmākam balam','<b>that army of ours</b>'),('bhīṣma-abhirakṣitam','<b>guarded by Bhīṣma</b>'),('paryāptam','<b>limited</b>, or <b>sufficient</b>'),('bhīma-abhirakṣitam','<b>guarded by Bhīma</b>')],
 'Duryodhana compares the two armies: eleven divisions against seven, Bhīṣma against Bhīma. Bhīma is not the Pāṇḍava commander, but he is the warrior Duryodhana fears most.',
 'The word <i>aparyāpta</i> has two opposite senses: “boundless” and “not enough”. Read one way, Duryodhana boasts; read the other way, he worries that his huge army, led by a grandfather who loves the Pāṇḍavas, will not be enough. Commentators take both readings. The double meaning suits a man who is outwardly proud and inwardly afraid.',
 'Size is not strength. A bigger team with a divided heart can lose to a smaller one with a single purpose.',
 'One word: <b>a boast or a fear</b>. Traditions differ.'),
X(11,'“ALL OF YOU, GUARD BHĪṢMA”',
 FS('Kaurava soldiers forming a ring around old Bhishma',SAGE(110,120,1.2)+''.join(SOLDIER(110+60*math.cos(k*.785),100+40*math.sin(k*.785),.5,KUR) for k in range(8))),
 '“So, all of you, standing firm in your places at every point of the line, protect Bhīṣma above all.”',
 [('ayaneṣu ca sarveṣu','<b>at every position</b>'),('yathā-bhāgam avasthitāḥ','<b>each in his place</b>'),('bhīṣmam eva','<b>Bhīṣma above all</b>'),('abhirakṣantu','<b>protect</b>'),('sarve eva hi','<b>all of you</b>')],
 'Duryodhana gives his only order of the chapter: guard Bhīṣma. Bhīṣma is old but nearly invincible; he had a boon to choose the time of his own death. While he stands, the Kauravas cannot lose.',
 'The order shows two things. Bhīṣma is the key to the Kaurava plan, and Duryodhana does not fully trust him, knowing he loves the Pāṇḍavas. In the epic Bhīṣma promised to fight but not to kill the Pāṇḍava brothers, and he would not fight Śikhaṇḍī. Guarding him is protecting the plan’s single point of failure.',
 'Every plan has a weak point. Good leaders name it honestly. Duryodhana knew his rested on one old man, which is why he worried so much.',
 'The whole plan rests <b>on one old man</b>.'),
X(12,'THE LION’S ROAR',
 FS('Old white-haired Bhishma lifting a conch to his lips, sound waves pouring out',SAGE(70,130,1.3)+CONCH(96,62,1.1)+''.join(f'<path d="M{120+k*14} {50-k*4} q10 20 0 40" fill="none" stroke="{PAN}" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(4))+T(150,24,'ROAR!',14)),
 'To cheer him, the grandsire of the Kurus, the mighty elder, roared like a lion and blew his conch.',
 [('tasya harṣam sañjanayan','<b>to give him joy</b>'),('kuru-vṛddhaḥ','<b>eldest of the Kurus</b>'),('pitāmahaḥ','<b>the grandsire</b>'),('siṃha-nādam vinadya','<b>roaring a lion’s roar</b>'),('śaṅkham dadhmau','<b>blew his conch</b>')],
 'Bhīṣma has heard Duryodhana’s nervous speech. He does not answer in words. He roars and blows his conch, the signal that battle has begun.',
 'Bhīṣma does it “to cheer him”, to lift Duryodhana’s spirits: kindness from an old man to a troubled grandson whose cause he does not believe in. Bhīṣma is bound by an old vow to serve the throne of Hastināpura, whoever sits on it. His roar is duty, not conviction.',
 'Sometimes we act from duty while our heart is elsewhere. Bhīṣma’s story asks whether old vows should outlast the good they were meant to serve.',
 'Bhīṣma answers worry <b>with a war cry</b>.'),
X(13,'THE NOISE OF WAR',
 FS('Drums, horns and conches all sounding together, the word tumult above',''.join(f'<rect x="{20+k*40}" y="100" width="26" height="30" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(5))+''.join(CONCH(30+k*40,70,.7) for k in range(5))+T(110,30,'TUMULT!',16)),
 'Then conches and kettledrums, tabors, drums and cow-horns all blared at once, and the sound was tremendous.',
 [('tataḥ','<b>then</b>'),('śaṅkhāḥ ca bheryaḥ ca','<b>conches and kettledrums</b>'),('paṇava-ānaka-gomukhāḥ','<b>tabors, drums, horns</b>'),('sahasā','<b>suddenly, all at once</b>'),('tumulaḥ','<b>tumultuous</b>')],
 'Bhīṣma’s conch sets off the whole Kaurava army. Every instrument of war sounds at once: conches, big drums (bherī), small drums (paṇava, ānaka) and horns (gomukha, “cow-mouth”).',
 'Sañjaya paints with sound. A battle in the epic began with noise, colour and banners, a performance as much as a fight. Notice: the Kaurava side makes a great noise, but no instrument is named for any hero except Bhīṣma. In the next verses each Pāṇḍava’s conch has its own name.',
 'Noise is not the same as strength. A crowd can be loud and directionless. Ask what is behind the noise.',
 'A wall of sound <b>from the Kauravas</b>.'),
X(14,'THE WHITE HORSES',
 FS('A white chariot with four white horses; Krishna and Arjuna both blowing conches',RATHA(90,140,.85)+CONCH(124,62,.8)+CONCH(64,68,.8)),
 'Then, standing in their great chariot yoked to white horses, Mādhava (Kṛṣṇa) and the son of Pāṇḍu (Arjuna) blew their divine conches.',
 [('śvetaiḥ hayaiḥ yukte','<b>yoked with white horses</b>'),('mahati syandane','<b>in a great chariot</b>'),('mādhavaḥ','<b>Mādhava</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('pāṇḍavaḥ','<b>the son of Pāṇḍu</b>: Arjuna'),('divyau śaṅkhau','<b>divine conches</b>')],
 'The Pāṇḍavas answer. First, from one chariot, Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna. Arjuna’s chariot was a gift from Agni, the fire god, with white horses and a banner bearing Hanumān.',
 'Sañjaya calls the conches <i>divya</i>, divine. On this side the answer is led by Kṛṣṇa himself. Later teachers often borrow the Kaṭha Upaniṣad’s picture: the chariot is the body, the horses the senses, the reins the mind, the driver the intellect, and the owner the Self. Here the Lord himself holds the reins.',
 'Who holds the reins of your chariot? The Kaṭha Upaniṣad says a wise driver keeps the senses like good horses on the road.',
 'Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna <b>answer together</b>.'),
X(15,'PĀÑCAJANYA AND DEVADATTA',
 FS('Krishna blowing his conch, Arjuna his conch, and Bhima his great conch',KR(50,120,.8)+CONCH(80,70,.8)+AR(110,124,.75)+CONCH(138,74,.8)+person(180,132,'#c0392b','#c98b5a',1.1)+CONCH(196,80,.9)),
 'Hṛṣīkeśa blew Pāñcajanya; Dhanañjaya, Devadatta; and Vṛkodara of terrible deeds blew the great conch Pauṇḍra.',
 [('hṛṣīkeśaḥ','<b>Lord of the senses</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('pāñcajanyam','<b>Pāñcajanya</b>, Kṛṣṇa’s conch'),('dhanañjayaḥ','<b>winner of wealth</b>: Arjuna'),('devadattam','<b>Devadatta</b>, “god-given”'),('vṛkodaraḥ','<b>wolf-bellied</b>: Bhīma')],
 'Each hero’s conch has a name. Kṛṣṇa took Pāñcajanya from the sea-demon Pañcajana. Arjuna’s Devadatta was a gift of the gods. Bhīma, the giant eater (“wolf-belly”), blows the great Pauṇḍra.',
 'Kṛṣṇa is called <i>Hṛṣīkeśa</i>, “master of the senses” (hṛṣīka). It is fitting: the Gītā will teach mastery of the senses, and its teacher is named for it here. Arjuna is <i>Dhanañjaya</i>, who won wealth for his brother’s great sacrifice. Names in the Gītā often carry a hint of the lesson.',
 'Names carry stories. In the Gītā Kṛṣṇa and Arjuna are called by dozens of names, each chosen for the moment. Notice what the names hint as you read.',
 'On this side, <b>Kṛṣṇa sounds first</b>.'),
X(16,'YUDHIṢṬHIRA AND THE TWINS',
 FS('King Yudhishthira and the twins Nakula and Sahadeva each blowing a conch',KING(50,130,1)+CONCH(74,74,.8)+person(120,130,'#2a9d6f','#c98b5a',1)+CONCH(140,80,.7)+person(180,130,PAN,'#c98b5a',1)+CONCH(200,80,.7)),
 'King Yudhiṣṭhira, son of Kuntī, blew Anantavijaya; Nakula and Sahadeva blew Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣpaka.',
 [('kuntī-putraḥ','<b>son of Kuntī</b>'),('yudhiṣṭhiraḥ','<b>steady in battle</b>: the eldest'),('anantavijayam','<b>“endless victory”</b>'),('sughoṣa','<b>“sweet-sounding”</b>'),('maṇipuṣpaka','<b>“jewel-flowered”</b>')],
 'The eldest Pāṇḍava, Yudhiṣṭhira, son of Dharma, blows Anantavijaya. The twins, sons of Mādrī, blow Sughoṣa and Maṇipuṣpaka. So all five brothers have sounded.',
 'Sañjaya calls Yudhiṣṭhira <i>rājā</i>, king, just as he called Duryodhana king. Two kings, one throne: that is the whole dispute. Yudhiṣṭhira was famous for truthfulness; his conch is named “endless victory”, a hint of where the story will go.',
 'Character outlasts power. Yudhiṣṭhira lost everything at dice, yet the epic honours him for his truthfulness, not his skill.',
 'Five brothers, <b>five conches</b>.'),
X(17,'THE ALLIES JOIN',
 FS('Allied kings in a row blowing conches',''.join(KING(24+k*42,130,.8)+CONCH(40+k*42,80,.6) for k in range(5))),
 'The king of Kāśī, the great archer; Śikhaṇḍī, the mighty chariot-warrior; Dhṛṣṭadyumna, Virāṭa and the unconquered Sātyaki;',
 [('kāśyaḥ','<b>the king of Kāśī</b>'),('parama-iṣvāsaḥ','<b>supreme archer</b>'),('śikhaṇḍī','<b>Śikhaṇḍī</b>'),('dhṛṣṭadyumnaḥ','<b>Dhṛṣṭadyumna</b>, the commander'),('aparājitaḥ','<b>unconquered</b>')],
 'The allies sound in turn. Śikhaṇḍī is key: born a girl, later a man, and in a former life the princess Ambā, whom Bhīṣma had wronged. Bhīṣma will not fight Śikhaṇḍī, and on the tenth day Arjuna will shoot him from behind Śikhaṇḍī.',
 'The epic plants its plot in this list. Dhṛṣṭadyumna will kill Droṇa; Śikhaṇḍī will bring down Bhīṣma. The two men Duryodhana relies on most each have a destined slayer on the other side. Sañjaya, telling this after Bhīṣma’s fall, knows it.',
 'Old wrongs return. Ambā’s grievance against Bhīṣma came back a lifetime later. What we do to others does not simply vanish.',
 'Every hero has <b>a destined opponent</b>.'),
X(18,'EVERY CONCH, ON EVERY SIDE',
 FS('Drupada, the sons of Draupadi and Abhimanyu blowing conches, sound waves rising',KING(40,130,1)+''.join(person(80+k*26,130,c,'#c98b5a',.7) for k,c in enumerate(['#c0392b','#2a9d6f','#1d6fb8','#ff8c1a','#ffd60a']))+''.join(f'<path d="M{60+k*30} 70 q10 -20 0 -40" fill="none" stroke="{PAN}" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(5))),
 'Drupada and the sons of Draupadī, O lord of the earth, and the mighty-armed son of Subhadrā: each blew his own conch, on every side.',
 [('drupadaḥ','<b>Drupada</b>'),('draupadeyāḥ','<b>the sons of Draupadī</b>'),('pṛthivī-pate','<b>O lord of the earth</b>: Dhṛtarāṣṭra'),('saubhadraḥ mahā-bāhuḥ','<b>Subhadrā’s mighty-armed son</b>'),('pṛthak pṛthak','<b>each his own</b>')],
 'The last of the Pāṇḍava side joins: Drupada, the five sons of Draupadī, and Abhimanyu. Sañjaya addresses the blind king as “lord of the earth”.',
 'The title is bitterly polite. Dhṛtarāṣṭra could have stopped all this with one word, by returning the Pāṇḍavas’ share. Sañjaya, his loyal minister, keeps gently reminding him of his power and his responsibility.',
 'Those with the power to stop a conflict carry its weight. Silence from the powerful is itself a decision.',
 '“Lord of the earth”: <b>he could have stopped it</b>.'),
X(19,'THE SOUND THAT TORE HEARTS',
 FS('Huge blue sound waves rolling across the field, purple soldiers clutching their chests',''.join(f'<path d="M{150-k*20} 30 q-14 50 0 100" fill="none" stroke="{PAN}" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(5))+ARMY(10,130,4,KUR,18,.55)),
 'That tumultuous sound, echoing through sky and earth, tore apart the hearts of the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra.',
 [('saḥ ghoṣaḥ','<b>that sound</b>'),('dhārtarāṣṭrāṇām','<b>of Dhṛtarāṣṭra’s side</b>'),('hṛdayāni vyadārayat','<b>tore their hearts</b>'),('nabhaḥ pṛthivīm ca','<b>sky and earth</b>'),('vyanunādayan','<b>making them echo</b>')],
 'The Pāṇḍava conches, though from a smaller army, shake the Kaurava side. The verse closes the opening scene of sound.',
 'Sañjaya says plainly to the blind king: your sons were shaken. Many readers see conscience at work: the side that knows it is in the wrong hears the other side’s call louder. Some editions read <i>tumulo’bhyanunādayan</i>; the meaning is the same.',
 'A clear conscience is a kind of strength. Doubt about one’s own cause weakens from within, however large the army.',
 'A clear conscience <b>sounds louder</b>.'),
X(20,'THE MONKEY BANNER',
 FS('Arjuna lifting his bow on the chariot under a banner, looking at the Kaurava ranks',ARMY(140,130,4,KUR,18,.55)+RATHA(70,146,.8)),
 'Then, seeing the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra drawn up, as the weapons were about to fly, Arjuna, whose banner bears the monkey, lifted his bow and, O king, spoke these words to Hṛṣīkeśa.',
 [('atha','<b>then</b>'),('vyavasthitān','<b>drawn up in position</b>'),('kapi-dhvajaḥ','<b>monkey-bannered</b>: Arjuna'),('pravṛtte śastra-sampāte','<b>as weapons were about to clash</b>'),('dhanuḥ udyamya','<b>raising his bow</b>')],
 'The armies stand ready; the first arrows are about to fly. Arjuna raises his great bow Gāṇḍīva. His banner bears Hanumān, who, in the epic, promised Bhīma to sit on Arjuna’s flag in the war.',
 'The monkey on the banner links the Mahābhārata to the Rāmāyaṇa: Hanumān, Rāma’s great devotee, now guards the chariot where Kṛṣṇa sits. At this moment Arjuna is fully a warrior, bow raised. In ten verses the bow will slip from his hand.',
 'Our certainties can change in a moment. Arjuna lifts the bow with confidence, then sees something that turns him upside down.',
 'Bow raised, <b>ready to begin</b>.'),
X(21,'“TO THE MIDDLE”',
 FS('Arjuna pointing to the space between the two armies',ARMY(4,130,3,KUR,16,.5)+ARMY(170,130,3,PAN,16,.5)+RATHA(100,146,.8)+BUBBLE(110,20,'TO THE MIDDLE!',100)),
 'Arjuna said: Acyuta, place my chariot between the two armies,',
 [('arjuna uvāca','<b>Arjuna said</b>'),('senayoḥ ubhayoḥ madhye','<b>between both armies</b>'),('ratham sthāpaya','<b>set the chariot</b>'),('me','<b>my</b>'),('acyuta','<b>O unfailing one</b>: Kṛṣṇa')],
 'Arjuna speaks for the first time. He asks Kṛṣṇa to drive into the open ground between the armies. The sentence runs on into the next verse.',
 'Arjuna gives an order and God obeys: the Lord of the universe drives for his friend. Arjuna calls him <i>Acyuta</i>, “the one who never slips”, the right name for a moment when Arjuna himself is about to slip. Devotees love this verse for its picture of God as a willing servant of those who love him.',
 'Before a big decision, Arjuna wanted to see clearly. Standing in the middle, between the options, is a good place to look from.',
 'God takes orders <b>from his friend</b>.'),
X(22,'“LET ME SEE THEM”',
 FS('Arjuna shading his eyes and scanning the Kaurava warriors',AR(60,110,1)+'<path d="M64 74 h20" stroke="#c98b5a" stroke-width="5"/>'+ARMY(110,130,5,KUR,20,.6)),
 '“…so that I may look at those standing here eager to fight, with whom I must fight in this undertaking of war;”',
 [('yāvat etān nirīkṣe','<b>so that I may look at these</b>'),('yoddhu-kāmān','<b>wanting to fight</b>'),('avasthitān','<b>standing ready</b>'),('kaiḥ mayā saha','<b>with whom I</b>'),('raṇa-samudyame','<b>in this undertaking of war</b>')],
 'Arjuna explains why: he wants to look at the men he must face, as a warrior sizes up his opponents.',
 'Arjuna expects to see enemies. What he will actually see is family. The Gītā is full of such turns: the eyes look for one thing and the heart finds another. Some readers say Kṛṣṇa let Arjuna make this request knowing what it would bring up.',
 'Looking closely at people often dissolves the label we gave them. “The enemy” is easier to fight than a face you know.',
 'He wants to see <b>his opponents</b>.'),
X(23,'“THOSE WHO PLEASE DURYODHANA”',
 FS('Arjuna frowning toward Duryodhana, who stands surrounded by followers',AR(50,110,1)+KING(160,130,1.1)+''.join(person(130+k*20,140,KUR,'#c98b5a',.5) for k in (0,3))),
 '“…I want to see those gathered here to fight, wishing to please in battle the evil-minded son of Dhṛtarāṣṭra.”',
 [('yotsyamānān','<b>those about to fight</b>'),('avekṣe','<b>I wish to see</b>'),('dhārtarāṣṭrasya','<b>of Dhṛtarāṣṭra’s son</b>'),('durbuddheḥ','<b>evil-minded</b>'),('priya-cikīrṣavaḥ','<b>wishing to please</b>')],
 'Arjuna still speaks as a warrior. He calls Duryodhana <i>durbuddhi</i>, “of bad understanding”, and the Kaurava allies men who came to please him.',
 'Arjuna has a clear story: the others are wrong, they serve a bad man, I will fight them. It is a confident moral picture, and it will not survive the next few verses. Strong certainty is sometimes the most fragile kind.',
 'We all hold simple stories about people we disagree with. Holding them lightly leaves room to see more.',
 'Arjuna is <b>still sure</b>, for now.'),
X(24,'THE CHARIOT IN THE MIDDLE',
 FS('The chariot halted in the open ground between the two armies',ARMY(4,130,3,KUR,16,.5)+ARMY(170,130,3,PAN,16,.5)+RATHA(100,146,.8)),
 'Sañjaya said: Thus addressed by Guḍākeśa, O Bhārata, Hṛṣīkeśa placed that finest of chariots between the two armies,',
 [('evam uktaḥ','<b>thus addressed</b>'),('guḍākeśena','<b>by Guḍākeśa</b>: Arjuna'),('hṛṣīkeśaḥ','<b>Hṛṣīkeśa</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('ratha-uttamam','<b>the finest chariot</b>'),('sthāpayitvā','<b>having placed</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa does as asked and drives into the middle. Sañjaya addresses the king as “Bhārata”, descendant of Bharata.',
 'A lovely pair of names: <i>Guḍākeśa</i>, “conqueror of sleep” (or “thick-haired”), and <i>Hṛṣīkeśa</i>, “master of the senses”. The one who has mastered sleep, alertness, and the one who has mastered the senses sit together in one chariot.',
 'Alertness and self-mastery belong together. One without the other leads astray.',
 'Kṛṣṇa drives <b>into the open ground</b>.'),
X(25,'“BEHOLD THE KURUS”',
 FS('Krishna turning to Arjuna and pointing toward old Bhishma and Drona',RATHA(80,146,.8)+SAGE(170,130,1)+SAGE(196,130,.9)+BUBBLE(150,24,'BEHOLD, PĀRTHA!',100)),
 '…in front of Bhīṣma, Droṇa and all the rulers of the earth, and said: “Pārtha, behold these Kurus gathered here.”',
 [('bhīṣma-droṇa-pramukhataḥ','<b>right in front of Bhīṣma and Droṇa</b>'),('mahī-kṣitām','<b>of the rulers of the earth</b>'),('uvāca','<b>he said</b>'),('pārtha','<b>son of Pṛthā</b> (Kuntī)'),('paśya etān kurūn','<b>behold these Kurus</b>')],
 'Kṛṣṇa stops exactly in front of Bhīṣma and Droṇa. Then he speaks his first words in the Gītā: “Behold the Kurus.”',
 'Kṛṣṇa could have stopped anywhere; he chose the spot facing Arjuna’s grandfather and teacher. And he says “Kurus”, Arjuna’s own family name, not “enemies”. Many teachers read this as deliberate: Kṛṣṇa brings the hidden conflict to the surface so that it can be healed. “Pārtha”, son of Pṛthā, reminds Arjuna of his mother, who is aunt to many on the other side.',
 'A good teacher sometimes lets us face the hard thing instead of avoiding it. Growth often starts with seeing clearly what hurts.',
 'Kṛṣṇa makes Arjuna <b>see his own family</b>.'),
X(26,'HE SAW HIS OWN',
 FS('Arjuna sees faces across the field: grandfathers, teachers, uncles, brothers, sons, friends',AR(40,110,.9,'sad')+''.join(person(100+k*22,124-(k%2)*10,c,'#c98b5a',.55) for k,c in enumerate(['#fff','#ff8c1a','#7a2a6a','#2a9d6f','#c0392b']))+T(150,30,'MY FAMILY',12)),
 'There Pārtha saw standing fathers and grandfathers, teachers, maternal uncles, brothers, sons, grandsons and friends;',
 [('apaśyat','<b>he saw</b>'),('pitṝn · pitāmahān','<b>fathers · grandfathers</b>'),('ācāryān','<b>teachers</b>'),('mātulān · bhrātṝn','<b>uncles · brothers</b>'),('putrān pautrān sakhīn','<b>sons, grandsons, friends</b>')],
 'Arjuna looks, and the list of relations begins. “Fathers” means elders of his father’s generation, such as Bhūriśravas; “grandfathers”, Bhīṣma and Somadatta; “sons”, the sons of his cousins.',
 'This is the wound of the Gītā. Arjuna came to see opponents and sees relations, one by one. The verse itself becomes a list, as if Sañjaya slows down to let each face register. Arjuna’s crisis is not cowardice; it is love colliding with duty.',
 'Behind every “them” are real people with names and families. Seeing that does not always change what we must do, but it changes how we do it.',
 'He came to see enemies <b>and saw family</b>.'),
X(27,'KIN ON BOTH SIDES',
 FS('Arjuna lowering his eyes, kinsmen on both sides of the field',AR(110,110,1.1,'sad')+''.join(person(20+k*20,140,KUR,'#c98b5a',.45) for k in range(3))+''.join(person(160+k*20,140,PAN,'#c98b5a',.45) for k in range(3))),
 '…fathers-in-law and well-wishers too, in both armies. Seeing all these kinsmen standing there, the son of Kuntī,',
 [('śvaśurān','<b>fathers-in-law</b>'),('suhṛdaḥ','<b>well-wishers</b>'),('senayoḥ ubhayoḥ api','<b>in both armies</b>'),('samīkṣya','<b>seeing fully</b>'),('sarvān bandhūn','<b>all the kinsmen</b>')],
 'The list ends with “in both armies”. Arjuna sees that his own side is full of family too. Every arrow, from either side, will strike someone dear.',
 'Sañjaya calls him <i>Kaunteya</i>, Kuntī’s son: a mother’s son, tender-hearted. The word <i>samīkṣya</i>, “seeing fully”, matters: he takes it all in. This complete seeing is what breaks him. The war is no longer an idea; it is faces.',
 'Sometimes truly seeing a situation is overwhelming. That is not weakness; it is the start of honest thought.',
 'Kin <b>on both sides</b>.'),
X(28,'OVERCOME WITH PITY',
 FS('Arjuna trembling, his face sad, the bow sagging in his hand',AR(100,110,1.1,'sad',False)+'<path d="M150 60 q-22 50 4 90" fill="none" stroke="#7a4a1a" stroke-width="5"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{70+k*8} 50 l-4 -10" stroke="#3b8fd0" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(6))),
 '…overcome with deep pity and sinking in sorrow, said: Arjuna said: Kṛṣṇa, seeing these my own people standing here eager to fight,',
 [('kṛpayā parayā āviṣṭaḥ','<b>overcome by deep pity</b>'),('viṣīdan','<b>sinking in sorrow</b>'),('arjuna uvāca','<b>Arjuna said</b>'),('sva-janam','<b>my own people</b>'),('yuyutsum samupasthitam','<b>gathered, eager to fight</b>')],
 'Sañjaya describes Arjuna’s state, then Arjuna speaks. The word <i>viṣīdan</i>, sinking in despair, gives chapter 1 its name: <i>viṣāda yoga</i>.',
 'Note the word <i>kṛpā</i>, pity or compassion. Is it a virtue here? In 2.2–3 Kṛṣṇa will call it the wrong kind: born of confusion and attachment, not of wisdom. Real compassion, the Gītā will teach, sees clearly. This is pity flooding a mind that has lost its footing.',
 'Feelings can be noble and still lead us astray. Kindness that comes from panic is different from kindness that comes from clarity.',
 'Pity floods <b>a troubled mind</b>.'),
X(29,'THE BODY BREAKS DOWN',
 FS('Arjuna shaking, hair on end, mouth dry',AR(110,110,1.4,'sad',False)+''.join(f'<path d="M{80+k*10} 40 l-2 -10" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(7))+T(110,150,'SHAKING',11)),
 '“…my limbs give way, my mouth is parched, my body trembles and my hair stands on end.”',
 [('sīdanti mama gātrāṇi','<b>my limbs fail</b>'),('mukham ca pariśuṣyati','<b>my mouth dries up</b>'),('vepathuḥ ca śarīre me','<b>trembling in my body</b>'),('roma-harṣaḥ','<b>hair standing on end</b>')],
 'Arjuna names his symptoms one by one, as if reporting to a doctor.',
 'The Gītā describes a panic or grief reaction with striking accuracy: weak limbs, dry mouth, trembling, goose-flesh. Indian tradition does not separate body and mind; the crisis shows in both. Kṛṣṇa will not treat the symptoms directly; he will treat the confusion behind them.',
 'Our bodies speak our feelings. A dry mouth or shaking hands before a hard moment is natural; it is a sign to slow down and look at what is going on inside.',
 'Grief shows <b>in the body</b>.'),
X(30,'GĀṆḌĪVA SLIPS',
 FS('The great bow Gandiva slipping from Arjuna’s hand, his head spinning',AR(80,110,1.1,'sad',False)+'<path d="M140 60 q-22 50 4 90" fill="none" stroke="#7a4a1a" stroke-width="5" transform="rotate(20 140 100)"/>'+T(170,40,'SLIP!',14)+'<path d="M60 40 q10 -10 20 0 q10 10 20 0" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'),
 '“Gāṇḍīva slips from my hand and my skin is burning. I cannot stand steady; my mind seems to reel.”',
 [('gāṇḍīvam','<b>Gāṇḍīva</b>, Arjuna’s bow'),('sraṃsate hastāt','<b>slips from my hand</b>'),('tvak paridahyate','<b>my skin burns</b>'),('na śaknomi avasthātum','<b>I cannot stand</b>'),('bhramati iva me manaḥ','<b>my mind seems to spin</b>')],
 'Gāṇḍīva was a divine bow, given by Agni, never failing in Arjuna’s hands. Now it slides from his grip.',
 'Arjuna was the greatest archer of his age; his identity is bound up with that bow. When the bow slips, his sense of who he is slips too. Many readers see here a moment everyone meets: when the role we built our life on no longer holds.',
 'When our usual strengths fail, it can feel as if the ground has gone. The Gītā suggests this is where a deeper strength can be found.',
 'The greatest archer <b>cannot hold his bow</b>.'),
X(31,'BAD OMENS',
 FS('Dark birds circling and a broken arrow; Arjuna looks up anxiously',AR(60,110,1,'sad',False)+''.join(f'<path d="M{110+k*24} {40+(k%2)*14} q6 -8 12 0 q6 -8 12 0" fill="none" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2.5"/>' for k in range(4))+'<path d="M120 140 l30 -20 M156 116 l20 -12" stroke="#7a5a3a" stroke-width="3"/>'),
 '“I see bad omens, Keśava, and I see no good in killing my own people in battle.”',
 [('nimittāni','<b>signs, omens</b>'),('viparītāni','<b>contrary, bad</b>'),('keśava','<b>Keśava</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('na ca śreyaḥ anupaśyāmi','<b>I see no good</b>'),('hatvā sva-janam','<b>killing my own people</b>')],
 'Arjuna now gives reasons. First: he sees bad omens. Then: no good can come from killing family.',
 'The word <i>śreyas</i>, the true good, is a key Gītā word. In 2.7 Arjuna will ask Kṛṣṇa what is <i>śreyas</i>; here he thinks he already knows. The word <i>nimitta</i> also returns in 11.33, where Kṛṣṇa calls Arjuna merely his instrument (<i>nimitta-mātram</i>). Chapter 1 plants words the Gītā will answer later.',
 'When afraid, we see signs everywhere that confirm the fear. It helps to ask: am I reading the situation, or reading my anxiety into it?',
 'Fear <b>reads omens everywhere</b>.'),
X(32,'“I DO NOT WANT VICTORY”',
 FS('Arjuna turning his back on a crown and treasure, shaking his head',AR(70,110,1,'sad',False)+'<path d="M140 90 h40 l-6 -20 l-7 8 l-7 -14 l-7 14 l-7 -8z" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+''.join(f'<circle cx="{146+k*10}" cy="118" r="5" fill="#e0a800" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(4))+T(160,148,'NO!',14)),
 '“I do not want victory, Kṛṣṇa, nor a kingdom, nor pleasures. What is a kingdom to us, Govinda, or enjoyment, or even life?”',
 [('na kāṅkṣe vijayam','<b>I do not want victory</b>'),('na ca rājyam sukhāni ca','<b>nor kingdom nor pleasures</b>'),('kim naḥ rājyena','<b>what is a kingdom to us?</b>'),('govinda','<b>Govinda</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('jīvitena vā','<b>or life itself</b>')],
 'Arjuna rejects the prize of war. Without his family, victory means nothing.',
 'These words sound like a saint’s, yet Kṛṣṇa will call them weakness. Why? The Gītā tests the motive. Arjuna gives up the kingdom because he cannot bear the pain, not because he has seen the truth. Real renunciation, the Gītā says later, comes from understanding, not from grief.',
 'Giving something up is not always wisdom. Ask whether you are letting go or running away.',
 'Giving up from grief <b>is not letting go</b>.'),
X(33,'FOR WHOSE SAKE?',
 FS('Arjuna gesturing toward his family across the field, an empty throne between them',AR(50,110,1,'sad',False)+'<rect x="100" y="80" width="40" height="40" fill="#ffd60a" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+T(120,74,'FOR WHOM?',10)+''.join(person(170+k*20,140,KUR,'#c98b5a',.5) for k in range(2))),
 '“Those for whose sake we desire kingdom, enjoyment and pleasures stand here in battle, giving up life and wealth:”',
 [('yeṣām arthe','<b>for whose sake</b>'),('kāṅkṣitam naḥ','<b>is desired by us</b>'),('rājyam bhogāḥ sukhāni ca','<b>kingdom, enjoyments, pleasures</b>'),('te ime','<b>these very people</b>'),('prāṇān dhanāni tyaktvā','<b>giving up life and wealth</b>')],
 'Arjuna’s logic: we want a kingdom to share with those we love. But they are the very people we must kill to get it.',
 'It is a sharp point, and the Gītā does not dismiss it. Wealth and power are hollow without the people they are for. Kṛṣṇa will answer from a deeper level: the question is not what Arjuna gains but what his duty is, and who he really is.',
 'Much of what we work for is meant to be shared. If chasing it destroys those relationships, we have lost the point.',
 'What good is a kingdom <b>without your people</b>?'),
X(34,'THE LIST OF THE DEAR',
 FS('A family portrait on the enemy side: teachers, fathers, sons, grandfathers, uncles, in-laws',''.join(person(24+k*22,130,c,'#c98b5a',.65) for k,c in enumerate(['#fff','#ff8c1a','#7a2a6a','#2a9d6f','#c0392b','#1d6fb8','#e0a800','#999']))+T(110,30,'ALL MY OWN',12)),
 '“teachers, fathers, sons, and grandfathers too; uncles, fathers-in-law, grandsons, brothers-in-law and other kinsmen.”',
 [('ācāryāḥ','<b>teachers</b>'),('pitaraḥ putrāḥ','<b>fathers, sons</b>'),('mātulāḥ śvaśurāḥ','<b>uncles, fathers-in-law</b>'),('śyālāḥ','<b>brothers-in-law</b>'),('sambandhinaḥ','<b>relatives</b>')],
 'Arjuna repeats the list of relations, as if he cannot stop.',
 'The repetition is itself a sign of distress: a mind circling the same pain. Kṛṣṇa lets him speak it all out. Good listening, before teaching, is part of the Gītā’s method.',
 'When someone is upset, they often say the same thing several times. Let them. It is how the mind starts to empty itself.',
 'A mind <b>circling its pain</b>.'),
X(35,'NOT EVEN FOR THREE WORLDS',
 FS('Arjuna pushing away three stacked worlds offered to him',AR(60,110,1,'sad',False)+''.join(f'<circle cx="160" cy="{40+k*30}" r="14" fill="{c}" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k,c in enumerate(['#ffd60a','#9fd3f0','#2a9d6f']))+T(160,148,'3 WORLDS',10)),
 '“These I do not wish to kill, Madhusūdana, even if they kill me; not even for rule over the three worlds, how much less for the earth.”',
 [('etān na hantum icchāmi','<b>I do not want to kill them</b>'),('ghnataḥ api','<b>even if they kill me</b>'),('madhusūdana','<b>slayer of Madhu</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('trailokya-rājyasya','<b>for rule of the three worlds</b>'),('mahī-kṛte','<b>for the sake of the earth</b>')],
 'Arjuna would rather be killed than kill his family, even if offered heaven, earth and the underworld.',
 'Arjuna calls Kṛṣṇa <i>Madhusūdana</i>, slayer of the demon Madhu. The irony is gentle: “you killed demons; but these are not demons, they are family.” Kṛṣṇa will in time show that the line between duty and attachment must be seen clearly, not drawn by feeling alone.',
 'Arjuna is ready to suffer rather than harm. That is noble. The Gītā’s question is whether it is also right, here and now.',
 'Arjuna would <b>rather die than kill</b>.'),
X(36,'WHAT JOY IN THIS?',
 FS('Arjuna looking at his hands, a small dark cloud of sin above',AR(90,110,1.1,'sad',False)+'<ellipse cx="160" cy="40" rx="40" ry="16" fill="#555" stroke="#111"/>'+T(160,44,'PĀPA',11).replace('fill="#111"','fill="#fff"')+T(160,120,'JOY? ✗',12)),
 '“What joy could we have, Janārdana, by killing the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra? Only sin would cling to us for killing these aggressors.”',
 [('nihatya','<b>having killed</b>'),('kā prītiḥ syāt','<b>what joy could there be?</b>'),('janārdana','<b>Janārdana</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('pāpam eva āśrayet','<b>only sin would cling</b>'),('ātatāyinaḥ','<b>aggressors</b>')],
 'Arjuna admits the Kauravas are <i>ātatāyin</i>, aggressors: those who set fire to homes, poison, attack with weapons, seize land and wives. The law-books allowed killing such people in self-defence.',
 'Arjuna knows the law permits it, yet he says sin would follow. He is weighing a legal duty against a moral feeling. The Gītā takes his dilemma seriously: it is a real conflict of dharmas, not a simple case. Kṛṣṇa will resolve it by changing the level of the question.',
 'What is legal and what feels right can differ. Wisdom is not ignoring either, but finding a higher view that does justice to both.',
 'Allowed by law, <b>forbidden by the heart</b>?'),
X(37,'“OUR OWN KIN”',
 FS('Arjuna with arms spread wide as if to shield both armies',AR(110,110,1.2,'sad',False)+'<path d="M80 100 h-50 M140 100 h50" stroke="#c98b5a" stroke-width="6"/>'+ARMY(4,140,2,KUR,16,.45)+ARMY(186,140,2,PAN,16,.45)),
 '“So we should not kill the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra, our own kinsmen. For how could we be happy, Mādhava, after killing our own people?”',
 [('tasmāt','<b>therefore</b>'),('na arhāḥ vayam hantum','<b>we ought not to kill</b>'),('sva-bāndhavān','<b>our own kinsmen</b>'),('sva-janam hi katham hatvā','<b>how, having killed our own</b>'),('sukhinaḥ syāma','<b>could we be happy?</b>')],
 'Arjuna draws his conclusion: we should not fight.',
 'Notice the word <i>sva</i>, “own”, which Arjuna repeats again and again. In verse 1 Dhṛtarāṣṭra said “mine” against “theirs”; Arjuna now says “ours” for everyone. It is a kinder word, yet still a kind of attachment. The Gītā will move him beyond both, to see the Self in all beings (6.29).',
 'Widening “mine” to “ours” is good. The Gītā’s final step is to see everyone as part of one Self, without possessiveness.',
 'From “mine” <b>to “ours”</b>.'),
X(38,'THEY CANNOT SEE',
 FS('Two Kaurava leaders with dark bands over their eyes, greed in a thought bubble',KING(80,130,1.1)+'<path d="M68 98 h24" stroke="#555" stroke-width="5"/>'+KING(140,130,1.1)+'<path d="M128 98 h24" stroke="#555" stroke-width="5"/>'+'<ellipse cx="110" cy="40" rx="50" ry="18" fill="#fff" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+T(110,44,'GREED',11)),
 '“Even if they, their minds overpowered by greed, see no wrong in destroying the family and no sin in betraying friends,”',
 [('yadi api ete na paśyanti','<b>even if they do not see</b>'),('lobha-upahata-cetasaḥ','<b>minds struck by greed</b>'),('kula-kṣaya-kṛtam doṣam','<b>the fault of destroying the family</b>'),('mitra-drohe ca pātakam','<b>the sin of betraying friends</b>')],
 'Arjuna admits the Kauravas are blinded by greed. They do not see the evil of this war. The sentence continues.',
 'An honest thought: the other side is wrong. But Arjuna argues that their blindness does not excuse ours. It is a strong moral point, often quoted: someone else’s wrong is no licence for mine.',
 'When others behave badly, we are tempted to match them. Arjuna’s instinct to hold a higher standard is right, even if his conclusion will change.',
 'Their blindness is <b>no excuse for ours</b>.'),
X(39,'WHY SHOULDN’T WE TURN BACK?',
 FS('Arjuna with wide-open eyes looking at a broken family tree',AR(60,110,1,'wow',False)+TREE(160,150,1.1)+'<path d="M150 70 l-14 -12 M172 72 l14 -10" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4"/>'),
 '“…why should we, who clearly see the wrong in destroying a family, not know to turn back from this sin, Janārdana?”',
 [('katham na jñeyam','<b>why should it not be known?</b>'),('asmābhiḥ','<b>by us</b>'),('pāpāt asmāt nivartitum','<b>to turn back from this sin</b>'),('kula-kṣaya-kṛtam doṣam','<b>the wrong of destroying a family</b>'),('prapaśyadbhiḥ','<b>by us who see clearly</b>')],
 'Arjuna completes the thought: they cannot see, but we can; so we must stop.',
 'Arjuna appeals to knowledge: we know better. The Gītā will agree that knowledge is everything, but it shows that Arjuna’s knowledge here is partial. He sees the family and its dharma; he does not yet see the Self, nor the larger dharma of resisting injustice. Partial sight, sincerely held, can lead astray.',
 'Being sure we see clearly is not the same as seeing clearly. A wise friend or teacher can show us what we are missing.',
 'He sees clearly, <b>but not fully</b>.'),
X(40,'WHEN FAMILY DHARMA DIES',
 FS('A family tree with broken branches; lamps going out below',TREE(60,140,1.2)+'<path d="M40 70 l-10 -10 M80 70 l12 -8" stroke="#c0392b" stroke-width="4"/>'+''.join(LAMP(130+k*24,140-k*4,.6) for k in range(3))+T(160,40,'DHARMA FADES',11)),
 '“When a family is destroyed, its age-old traditions perish; when dharma perishes, lawlessness overcomes the whole family.”',
 [('kula-kṣaye','<b>in the destruction of a family</b>'),('praṇaśyanti','<b>perish</b>'),('kula-dharmāḥ sanātanāḥ','<b>the ancient family traditions</b>'),('dharme naṣṭe','<b>when dharma is lost</b>'),('adharmaḥ abhibhavati','<b>lawlessness overpowers</b>')],
 'Arjuna moves from personal grief to a social argument. Families carry rites, customs and values; kill the elders and those traditions die.',
 'In Arjuna’s world, <i>kula-dharma</i> meant the rites for ancestors, customs of marriage, duties to elders. With the men dead, who would keep them? It is the argument of a thoughtful traditional man, and Kṛṣṇa does not mock it. He will set it within a larger dharma.',
 'Traditions live through people. When a generation is lost, to war, migration or neglect, much quietly disappears. Arjuna’s worry is real.',
 'Arjuna argues <b>from the dharma of family</b>.'),
X(41,'WHEN ORDER BREAKS',
 sc('A house in disarray with a broken door and scattered people',SKY+'<path d="M60 150 V90 L110 50 L160 90 V150z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/><path d="M100 150 V110 h20 V150" fill="#555"/>'+''.join(person(30+k*60,150,'#999','#c98b5a',.45) for k in range(3))),
 '“When lawlessness prevails, Kṛṣṇa, the women of the family go astray; and when women go astray, O Vārṣṇeya, there is mixing of social orders.”',
 [('adharma-abhibhavāt','<b>when lawlessness prevails</b>'),('praduṣyanti kula-striyaḥ','<b>the family’s women are led astray</b>'),('vārṣṇeya','<b>descendant of Vṛṣṇi</b>: Kṛṣṇa'),('varṇa-saṅkaraḥ','<b>mixing of social orders</b>')],
 'Arjuna continues his chain: with the men dead and dharma gone, women would be unprotected and exploited, and the social order would break down.',
 'This verse reflects the social views of its time, when lineage and the protection of women were tied to order. Modern readers may find parts of it hard. Many read its core concern as the plight of widows and orphans after war, which was real. The Gītā presents it as Arjuna’s argument, not Kṛṣṇa’s teaching.',
 'War leaves the most vulnerable unprotected. Whatever our view of ancient social rules, the fear for those left behind is very human.',
 'War leaves <b>the vulnerable unprotected</b>.'),
X(42,'THE ANCESTORS FALL',
 sc('Offerings of rice balls left unattended, ancestor spirits drifting away',SKY+''.join(f'<circle cx="{40+k*30}" cy="140" r="8" fill="#fff8ec" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(4))+''.join(GLOW(50+k*40,50,12,'#9fd3f0',.7) for k in range(4))+T(110,100,'NO OFFERINGS',10)),
 '“This mixing leads to hell both the family and those who destroyed it; for their ancestors fall, deprived of the offerings of rice-balls and water.”',
 [('saṅkaraḥ','<b>mixing, confusion</b>'),('narakāya eva','<b>to hell itself</b>'),('kula-ghnānām','<b>of the family-destroyers</b>'),('patanti pitaraḥ','<b>the ancestors fall</b>'),('lupta-piṇḍa-udaka-kriyāḥ','<b>deprived of rice-ball and water rites</b>')],
 'In the old belief, ancestors are sustained by śrāddha offerings of piṇḍa (rice-balls) and water, given by descendants. If the family line is broken, the ancestors suffer.',
 'Arjuna’s fear reaches the dead. Even today many Hindu families perform śrāddha and tarpaṇa for ancestors. Arjuna feels responsible for the living, the unborn and the departed. Kṛṣṇa will answer at a different level: the Self is not bound to any one body or family line.',
 'Remembering ancestors connects us to where we came from. The Gītā keeps that honour, while teaching that each soul’s journey is its own.',
 'His fear reaches <b>even the ancestors</b>.'),
X(43,'THE AGE-OLD RULES',
 sc('A crumbling stone tablet of traditions',SKY+'<rect x="60" y="30" width="100" height="110" rx="10" fill="#c8c8c8" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M72 {50+k*16} h76" stroke="#777" stroke-width="3"/>' for k in range(5))+'<path d="M110 30 l-10 40 l14 20 l-12 50" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/>'),
 '“By these faults of the family-destroyers, which bring about the mixing of orders, the age-old duties of community and family are destroyed.”',
 [('doṣaiḥ etaiḥ','<b>by these faults</b>'),('kula-ghnānām','<b>of family-destroyers</b>'),('varṇa-saṅkara-kārakaiḥ','<b>causing the mixing of orders</b>'),('utsādyante','<b>are destroyed</b>'),('jāti-dharmāḥ kula-dharmāḥ ca śāśvatāḥ','<b>the lasting duties of community and family</b>')],
 'Arjuna sums up: destroying the family destroys community and family duties alike.',
 'Arjuna calls these customs <i>śāśvata</i>, eternal. In chapter 2 Kṛṣṇa will speak of what is truly eternal: the Self. One quiet lesson of the Gītā is to sort what is truly lasting from what only feels permanent. Some customs change with time; the Self does not.',
 'Many things we call “eternal” are habits of a time and place. Wisdom knows which to keep and which can change.',
 'What does <b>“eternal”</b> really mean?'),
X(44,'“SO WE HAVE HEARD”',
 FS('Arjuna remembering an elder telling him stories, a dark place in a thought bubble',AR(50,110,1,'sad',False)+SAGE(110,130,.9)+'<ellipse cx="170" cy="50" rx="40" ry="24" fill="#3a1414" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+T(170,54,'NARAKA',10).replace('fill="#111"','fill="#fff"')),
 '“For people whose family duties are destroyed, Janārdana, a dwelling in hell is certain; so we have heard.”',
 [('utsanna-kula-dharmāṇām','<b>whose family dharma is ruined</b>'),('manuṣyāṇām','<b>of people</b>'),('narake niyatam vāsaḥ','<b>a fixed stay in hell</b>'),('bhavati','<b>there is</b>'),('iti anuśuśruma','<b>so we have heard</b>')],
 'Arjuna appeals to what elders taught him: those who destroy family dharma go to hell.',
 '“So we have heard” is honest: Arjuna relies on tradition, not on his own seeing. In 4.34 Kṛṣṇa will say: learn from those who have <i>seen</i> the truth. The Gītā values tradition but asks us to understand it from within.',
 'Received wisdom is a starting point, not an endpoint. The Gītā invites us to test and understand what we have heard.',
 'Relying <b>on what he has heard</b>.'),
X(45,'“ALAS, WHAT A GREAT SIN”',
 FS('Arjuna holding his head as a dark cloud labelled greed hangs over both armies',AR(110,110,1.1,'sad',False)+'<ellipse cx="110" cy="34" rx="70" ry="18" fill="#555" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+T(110,38,'GREED FOR A KINGDOM',10).replace('fill="#111"','fill="#fff"')),
 '“Alas! What a great sin we are set on committing, ready to kill our own people out of greed for the pleasures of a kingdom.”',
 [('aho bata','<b>alas!</b>'),('mahat pāpam','<b>a great sin</b>'),('vyavasitāḥ','<b>resolved, set on</b>'),('rājya-sukha-lobhena','<b>by greed for the pleasures of rule</b>'),('hantum sva-janam','<b>to kill our own people</b>')],
 'Arjuna turns on himself: we too are guilty, driven by greed for a kingdom.',
 'Arjuna has decided the moral question alone, in shock, without consulting anyone. He calls the Pāṇḍavas greedy, though they had asked for only five villages. Grief exaggerates. In chapter 2 he will finally do the wise thing and ask for guidance.',
 'Strong emotions push us to sweeping verdicts about others or ourselves. It helps to wait, and to ask someone wise before deciding.',
 'A verdict <b>reached in shock</b>.'),
X(46,'“LET THEM KILL ME”',
 FS('Arjuna sitting unarmed with open hands as armed Kaurava soldiers approach',AR(70,120,1,'sad',False)+ARMY(120,130,4,KUR,22,.65)+T(110,30,'UNARMED, UNRESISTING',10)),
 '“Better for me if the sons of Dhṛtarāṣṭra, weapons in hand, were to kill me in battle, unarmed and unresisting.”',
 [('yadi mām apratīkāram','<b>if me, not resisting</b>'),('aśastram','<b>unarmed</b>'),('śastra-pāṇayaḥ','<b>weapons in hand</b>'),('raṇe hanyuḥ','<b>should kill in battle</b>'),('tat me kṣemataram bhavet','<b>that would be better for me</b>')],
 'Arjuna’s last word in chapter 1: he would rather be killed unarmed than fight.',
 'Some hear nonviolence here; others hear despair. Gandhi, a great lover of the Gītā, read the whole war as allegory and drew nonviolence from the book as a whole. But within the story Kṛṣṇa will treat Arjuna’s wish not as a principle but as collapse: a warrior abandoning the people he is meant to protect.',
 'Refusing to act can be noble, or a way to escape a hard duty. The difference lies in what moves us: clear conviction, or fear and grief.',
 'Despair <b>dressed as peace</b>?'),
X(47,'HE SANK DOWN ON THE SEAT',
 FS('Arjuna slumped in the chariot, bow and arrows on the floor, Krishna looking at him quietly',RATHA(110,146,.95,kr=True,ar=False)+AR(76,120,.7,'sad',False)+'<path d="M40 150 q-6 -30 10 -46" fill="none" stroke="#7a4a1a" stroke-width="4"/>'+''.join(f'<path d="M{50+k*6} 150 l18 -6" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>' for k in range(4))),
 'Sañjaya said: Having spoken thus on the battlefield, Arjuna cast aside his bow and arrows and sank down on the seat of the chariot, his mind overwhelmed with grief.',
 [('evam uktvā','<b>having spoken thus</b>'),('saṅkhye','<b>on the battlefield</b>'),('ratha-upasthe upāviśat','<b>sat down on the chariot seat</b>'),('visṛjya sa-śaram cāpam','<b>casting aside bow and arrows</b>'),('śoka-saṃvigna-mānasaḥ','<b>mind shaken by grief</b>')],
 'Chapter 1 ends in silence. Kṛṣṇa has spoken only one line. Arjuna sits down, weapons dropped.',
 'The chapter is called <i>Arjuna Viṣāda Yoga</i>, the yoga of Arjuna’s despair. Why call despair a yoga? Because this collapse is the doorway: without it there would be no question, and no Gītā. Many teachers say every spiritual life begins with a moment like this, when the old answers stop working.',
 'Low moments are not wasted. When life drops us on the chariot floor, it is often the start of real learning, if we turn and ask.',
 'The teaching begins <b>where Arjuna breaks</b>.',
 ("Despair is a yoga?","When the old certainties fail, a person is finally ready to learn. Arjuna's breakdown is the doorway to the Gītā.")),
]
cover=('<svg width="380" height="268" viewBox="0 0 380 268" role="img" aria-label="A white chariot with four white horses and a monkey banner stands between two great armies; Krishna holds the reins while Arjuna slumps with his bow lowered">'
 '<rect width="380" height="268" fill="#0b2a4a" rx="8"/>'+GLOW(190,120,110,'#ffd60a',.18)+'<path d="M0 220 h380 V268 H0z" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111"/>'
 +ARMY(6,226,5,KUR,22,.75)+ARMY(268,226,5,PAN,22,.75)+RATHA(170,234,1.35,armood='sad',arbow=False)+'</svg>')
st0=textpage('setting','HOW DID IT COME TO THIS?','The story before the Gītā',
 panels('Three panels: a game of dice in a royal hall; the Pandava brothers walking into the forest in exile; Krishna choosing to be Arjuna’s unarmed charioteer',[
  ('#fde4e4','THE DICE','<rect x="40" y="90" width="66" height="40" fill="#c9a26b" stroke="#111" stroke-width="2"/>'+''.join(f'<rect x="{52+k*16}" y="80" width="10" height="10" fill="#fff" stroke="#111"/>' for k in range(3))+KING(30,150,.7)+KING(116,150,.7)),
  ('#e3f2fd','13 YEARS OF EXILE',TREE(30,170,.7)+TREE(120,170,.7)+''.join(person(40+k*16,170,c,'#c98b5a',.45) for k,c in enumerate(['#fff','#ff8c1a','#c0392b','#2a9d6f','#1d6fb8']))),
  ('#fff3b0','“I WON’T FIGHT”',KR(72,120,.75))])
 +'<div class="panel" style="margin-top:12px"><span class="caption">The story</span><div class="steps" style="margin:0">'
 +''.join(f'<div>{s}</div>' for s in ['<b>Two families, one throne.</b> The blind king Dhṛtarāṣṭra rules Hastināpura. His hundred sons, led by Duryodhana, are the Kauravas. His dead brother Pāṇḍu’s five sons, Yudhiṣṭhira, Bhīma, Arjuna, Nakula and Sahadeva, are the Pāṇḍavas.',
  '<b>The dice.</b> Duryodhana’s uncle Śakuni wins the Pāṇḍavas’ kingdom at a rigged game of dice. Their wife Draupadī is dragged into the court and insulted.',
  '<b>Exile.</b> The Pāṇḍavas spend twelve years in the forest and a thirteenth in hiding. When they return, Duryodhana will not give back even five villages.',
  '<b>Kṛṣṇa’s choice.</b> Kṛṣṇa offers one side his army and the other side himself, unarmed. Arjuna chooses Kṛṣṇa, who becomes his charioteer.',
  '<b>The divine eye.</b> Vyāsa gives Sañjaya, the king’s minister, the power to see the whole battle from the palace. The Gītā is Sañjaya telling the blind king what he sees.'])+'</div></div>',
 ("So this is a story inside a story?","Yes. Sañjaya tells Dhṛtarāṣṭra what Kṛṣṇa told Arjuna. The Gītā is 700 verses inside the Bhīṣma Parva of the Mahābhārata."),
 'The Gītā is spoken <b>on the eve of a family war</b>.')
P=dict(ch=1,nv=47,V=V,per=[14,17,16],out='/home/claude/work/gita/Gita_Chapter01.html',
 title='Arjuna’s Despair · Bhagavad Gītā Chapter 1 · srini.presents',
 desc='Bhagavad Gītā chapter 1, Arjuna Viṣāda Yoga, every śloka explained: the armies at Kurukṣetra, the conches, Arjuna’s chariot between the armies and his collapse into grief. All 47 ślokas in IAST, each on two pages with meaning, key words and a deep dive.',
 burst='CHAPTER 1 OF 18',h1='ARJUNA’S DESPAIR',series='BHAGAVAD GĪTĀ · CHAPTER 1 · ARJUNA VIṢĀDA YOGA',tag='Two armies, one chariot, one broken man · every śloka explained',cover=cover,
 where=[f'The {OVL} sets the scene: the Gītā is 700 verses in 18 chapters, a conversation between Kṛṣṇa and the warrior Arjuna on the battlefield of the Mahābhārata.',
  'Chapter 1 has almost no teaching. It is a scene: two armies, conches, a chariot driven between them, and a hero who suddenly sees his own family across the field and cannot go on. The rest of the Gītā answers this one chapter. Here every śloka gets two pages.'],
 wtalk=("A whole chapter with no teaching?","The question comes first. Without Arjuna's crisis there would be no Gītā. Watch how a strong man falls apart."),
 fullnote=FULLNOTE,
 secs=[('THE SETTING',1),('THE ROLL CALL',2),('THE CONCHES',12),('THE CHARIOT',20),('ARJUNA BREAKS',28),('ARJUNA’S ARGUMENTS',31)],
 story={1:[('setting','How Did It Come to This?',st0)]},
 end=['<b>The question (1.1).</b> The blind king asks what “my sons and Pāṇḍu’s sons” did. Sañjaya, with divine sight, tells him.',
  '<b>The roll call (1.2–11).</b> Duryodhana anxiously names the heroes of both sides to Droṇa.',
  '<b>The conches (1.12–19).</b> Bhīṣma roars; Kṛṣṇa, Arjuna and every Pāṇḍava answer, and the sound shakes the Kauravas.',
  '<b>The chariot (1.20–27).</b> Arjuna asks Kṛṣṇa to drive between the armies, and sees grandfathers, teachers and kin.',
  '<b>The collapse (1.28–47).</b> His body fails; he refuses victory, fears for family dharma, and drops his bow.'],
 endsub='Chapter 1 in five lines',
 next=("What's next?",f'{chl(2)}: Sāṅkhya Yoga. Arjuna becomes a student and asks Kṛṣṇa for help. The teaching begins: the Self that is never born and never dies, and work without craving for its fruit.'),
 endkey='<b>the Gītā begins where a hero breaks down</b>.',
 foot=FOOT(1,'Back-story details (dice, exile, Kṛṣṇa’s choice, Sañjaya’s divine eye, army sizes, later events of the war) come from the wider Mahābhārata, not from chapter 1 itself. '))
